{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-124003" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y)=y{e}^{2x}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨t,3{t}^{2}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-124003" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(1,3).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(1,3)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =(3{e}^{2\\cdot 1})-(0\\cdot {e}^{2(0)}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{2}-0 \\\\  & =3{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-124003" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "22b56597249b6b35237e227b0cc72694c8c31c102eb1f5ccb0fd40bc73e0e58e"} {"question" "q-124003" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-124003" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3+e"] ["math" "3e"] ["math" "3e+1"] ["math" "3{e}^{2}"] ["math" "{e}^{2}-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-124003" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3{e}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-124008" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y,z)={e}^{x}+{e}^{y}+{e}^{z}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨t,{t}^{2},2{t}^{3}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-124008" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0,0⟩ \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,1,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0,0)$ and $(1,1,2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(1,1,2)-f(0,0,0) \\\\  & =({e}^{1}+{e}^{1}+{e}^{2})-({e}^{0}+{e}^{0}+{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =(2e+{e}^{2})-3 \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+2e-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-124008" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "22b56597249b6b35237e227b0cc72694c8c31c102eb1f5ccb0fd40bc73e0e58e"} {"question" "q-124008" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-124008" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3e-3"] ["math" "3{e}^{2}-2"] ["math" "{e}^{2}+2e-3"] ["math" "{e}^{2}+3e"] ["math" "{e}^{2}-2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-124008" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{2}+2e-3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-124005" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+x.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨t,2t⟩$ for $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-124005" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ⟩ \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,2\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )$ and $(\\pi ,2\\pi ).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,2\\pi )-f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ) \\\\  & =(2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +\\pi )-(\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-124005" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "22b56597249b6b35237e227b0cc72694c8c31c102eb1f5ccb0fd40bc73e0e58e"} {"question" "q-124005" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-124005" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2\\pi "] ["math" "-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-124005" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-124002" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+x+y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨\\sqrt{t},t+1⟩$ for $t\\in [0,4],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-124002" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,1⟩ \\\\ r(4) & =⟨2,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(2,5).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(4))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(2,5)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =({2}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2}+2+5)-({0}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}+0+1) \\\\  & =107-1 \\\\  & =106.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-124002" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "22b56597249b6b35237e227b0cc72694c8c31c102eb1f5ccb0fd40bc73e0e58e"} {"question" "q-124002" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-124002" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "106"] ["math" "109"] ["math" "76"] ["math" "87"] ["math" "98"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-124002" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "106"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-104623" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y-{y}^{2}x.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi ],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-104623" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\pi ,2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,2)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =({\\pi }^{2}\\cdot 2-{2}^{2}\\cdot \\pi )-({0}^{2}\\cdot 0-{0}^{2}\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =(2{\\pi }^{2}-4\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =2{\\pi }^{2}-4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-104623" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "22b56597249b6b35237e227b0cc72694c8c31c102eb1f5ccb0fd40bc73e0e58e"} {"question" "q-104623" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-104623" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "2{\\pi }^{2}-4\\pi "] ["math" "\\pi +2"] ["math" "{\\pi }^{2}+2\\pi "] ["math" "{\\pi }^{2}-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-104623" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2{\\pi }^{2}-4\\pi "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-124030" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{e}^{xy}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $\\frac{x}{2}=\\sqrt{y}$ for $y\\in [1,4],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-124030" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\sqrt{t},t⟩,\\,t\\in [1,4].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨2,1⟩, \\\\ r(4) & =⟨4,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(4))-f(r(1)) \\\\  & =f(4,4)-f(2,1) \\\\  & =({4}^{2}+{e}^{4\\cdot 4})-({2}^{2}+{e}^{2\\cdot 1}) \\\\  & =(16+{e}^{16})-(4+{e}^{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{16}-{e}^{2}+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-124030" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "22b56597249b6b35237e227b0cc72694c8c31c102eb1f5ccb0fd40bc73e0e58e"} {"question" "q-124030" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-124030" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "16-{e}^{16}"] ["math" "{e}^{16}+16"] ["math" "{e}^{16}+{e}^{4}-4"] ["math" "{e}^{16}-{e}^{2}+12"] ["math" "{e}^{4}+12"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-124030" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{16}-{e}^{2}+12"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-124035" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y)={y}^{2}-x.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ for $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-124035" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,0⟩, \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨-1,\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(-1,\\pi )-f(1,0) \\\\  & =({\\pi }^{2}-(-1))-({0}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =({\\pi }^{2}+1)+1 \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-124035" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "22b56597249b6b35237e227b0cc72694c8c31c102eb1f5ccb0fd40bc73e0e58e"} {"question" "q-124035" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-124035" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi +1"] ["math" "\\pi -2"] ["math" "{\\pi }^{2}+2"] ["math" "{\\pi }^{2}-\\pi "] ["math" "{\\pi }^{2}-\\pi +1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-124035" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\pi }^{2}+2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-104617" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y=2x$ for  $x\\in [0,\\pi ],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-104617" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,2t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,2\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\pi ,2\\pi ).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,2\\pi )-f(0,0) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi )-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =(0+1)-(0+1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-104617" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "22b56597249b6b35237e227b0cc72694c8c31c102eb1f5ccb0fd40bc73e0e58e"} {"question" "q-104617" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-104617" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "\\pi "] ["math" "\\pi +1"] ["math" "\\pi -1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-104617" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-123879" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y)={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve ${y}^{2}=4x$ for $y\\in [0,2\\sqrt{2}],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-123879" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4},t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\sqrt{2}].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(2\\sqrt{2}) & =⟨2,2\\sqrt{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2\\sqrt{2}))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(2,2\\sqrt{2})-f(0,0) \\\\  & =((2\\sqrt{2})^{2}-{2}^{2})-({0}^{2}-{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =(8-4)-(0) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-123879" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "22b56597249b6b35237e227b0cc72694c8c31c102eb1f5ccb0fd40bc73e0e58e"} {"question" "q-123879" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-123879" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-123879" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-124029" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve ${y}^{2}=x$ for $y\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-124029" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{t}^{2},t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2})-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\cdot 1-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-124029" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "22b56597249b6b35237e227b0cc72694c8c31c102eb1f5ccb0fd40bc73e0e58e"} {"question" "q-124029" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-124029" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+1"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{4}+1"] ["math" "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}-4"] ["math" "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}"] ["math" "{\\pi }^{2}+2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-124029" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-104761" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=2x{e}^{-y}\\,i+(2y-{x}^{2}{e}^{-y})\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$ for $x\\in [2,3].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-104761" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={{2x{e}^{-y}}_{⏟}}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+{{(2y-{x}^{2}{e}^{-y})}_{⏟}}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-2x{e}^{-y}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =2x{e}^{-y}, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =2y-{x}^{2}{e}^{-y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}{e}^{-y}+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-1)⟩,\\,t\\in [2,3].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =⟨2,0⟩, \\\\ r(3) & =⟨3,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(2,0)$ and $(3,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(3,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-f(2,0) \\\\  & =({3}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2})-({2}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{-0}+{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡2-4 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-104761" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "22b56597249b6b35237e227b0cc72694c8c31c102eb1f5ccb0fd40bc73e0e58e"} {"question" "q-104761" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-104761" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1+{ln}^{2}⁡3"] ["math" "1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡2"] ["math" "\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡2}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-104761" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-104733" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,{t}^{2}⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for $F=({x}^{2}-y)\\,i+({y}^{2}-x)\\,j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-104733" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ Since we're given that $F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & ={x}^{2}-y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & ={y}^{2}-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}-xy.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨0,1⟩, \\\\ r(2) & =⟨3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {4}^{3}-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot 4)-(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {0}^{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {1}^{3}-0\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =9{ln}^{3}⁡2+\\frac{64}{3}-12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =9{ln}^{3}⁡2-12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+21.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-104733" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "22b56597249b6b35237e227b0cc72694c8c31c102eb1f5ccb0fd40bc73e0e58e"} {"question" "q-104733" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-104733" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "9{ln}^{3}⁡2-12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+21"] ["math" "{ln}^{2}⁡2+9"] ["math" "{ln}^{3}⁡2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-9"] ["math" "{ln}^{3}⁡2-63"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-104733" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "9{ln}^{3}⁡2-12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+21"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-104747" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the curve $y={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ for $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-104747" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}_{⏟}}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+{{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,2⟩, \\\\ r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi }{2},e⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},e).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},e)-f(0,2) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡e)-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡2) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡e-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡2 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-104747" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "22b56597249b6b35237e227b0cc72694c8c31c102eb1f5ccb0fd40bc73e0e58e"} {"question" "q-104747" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-104747" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡e-1"] ["math" "\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(e-1)"] ["math" "\\operatorname{sin}⁡2e-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{e}^{2}"] ["math" "\\operatorname{sin}⁡e"] ["math" "\\operatorname{sin}⁡e-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-104747" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\operatorname{sin}⁡e"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-104745" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=({e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,i+(x{e}^{y}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the curve $y=x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-104745" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={{({e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+{{(x{e}^{y}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{y}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & ={e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =x{e}^{y}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x{e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,1⟩, \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,\\pi -1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(\\pi ,\\pi -1).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,\\pi -1)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =(\\pi \\cdot {e}^{\\pi -1}+(\\pi -1)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )-(0\\cdot {e}^{1}+1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =(\\pi {e}^{\\pi -1}+0)-0 \\\\  & =\\pi {e}^{\\pi -1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-104745" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "22b56597249b6b35237e227b0cc72694c8c31c102eb1f5ccb0fd40bc73e0e58e"} {"question" "q-104745" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-104745" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi "] ["math" "\\pi {e}^{\\pi -1}"] ["math" "{\\pi }^{2}+1"] ["math" "{e}^{\\pi }+1"] ["math" "{e}^{{\\pi }^{2}-1}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-104745" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\pi {e}^{\\pi -1}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-104739" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,i+(2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{y})\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the curve $y={x}^{2}+{sin}^{2}⁡x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-104739" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={{{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}_{⏟}}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+{{(2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{y})}_{⏟}}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & ={y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{y}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{t}^{2}+{sin}^{2}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,{\\pi }^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\pi ,{\\pi }^{2}).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,{\\pi }^{2})-f(0,0) \\\\  & =({\\pi }^{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +{e}^{{\\pi }^{2}})-({0}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0+{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =(0+{e}^{{\\pi }^{2}})-(0+1) \\\\  & ={e}^{{\\pi }^{2}}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-104739" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "22b56597249b6b35237e227b0cc72694c8c31c102eb1f5ccb0fd40bc73e0e58e"} {"question" "q-104739" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-104739" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "{\\pi }^{2}"] ["math" "{e}^{2}+\\pi "] ["math" "{e}^{\\pi -1}"] ["math" "{e}^{{\\pi }^{2}}-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "q-104739" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{{\\pi }^{2}}-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/state.json" "file_sha256" "14d61f6054fea98ab01d249f59e3116617f024754db721a62b1522ec4bd371e7"} {"question" "e-8759" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y,z)=xy^2+ 2xz.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $\\mathbf r(t)= \\langle e^{t}, t, e^{-t}\\rangle$ for $t\\in \\left[0,1\\right],$ evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_{C} \\nabla f\\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/example-8759.json" "file_sha256" "db938e7491371c9012b0d1981ac80442f83995b1de5147bdbb412ce698a82bb7"} {"question" "e-8759" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,0,1⟩ \\\\ r(1) & =⟨e,1,\\frac{1}{e}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,0,1)$ and $\\left(e, 1, \\dfrac{1}{e}\\right).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(e,1,\\frac{1}{e})-f(1,0,1) \\\\  & =(e(1)^{2}+2\\cdot e\\cdot \\frac{1}{e})-(1(0)^{2}+2(1)(1)) \\\\  & =(e+2)-2 \\\\  & =e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/example-8759.json" "file_sha256" "db938e7491371c9012b0d1981ac80442f83995b1de5147bdbb412ce698a82bb7"} {"question" "e-9502" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y)= xe^{y}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $x + 2y=3$ for $x\\in [-1,1],$ evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_{C} \\nabla f\\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/example-9502.json" "file_sha256" "a23a739c188e2817d745868958ab81ea29a1e6670cdf9099d8f60478cf0103cc"} {"question" "e-9502" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\left\\langle t, \\dfrac{1}{2}(3-t)\\right\\rangle, \\quad t\\in[-1,1].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =⟨-1,2⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(-1,2)$ and $(1,1).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(-1)) \\\\  & =f(1,1)-f(-1,2) \\\\  & =1\\cdot {e}^{1}-(-1)\\cdot {e}^{2} \\\\  & =e+{e}^{2} \\\\  & =e(1+e).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/example-9502.json" "file_sha256" "a23a739c188e2817d745868958ab81ea29a1e6670cdf9099d8f60478cf0103cc"} {"question" "e-8760" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\left\\langle t,  1-t \\right\\rangle, \\quad 0 \\leq t \\leq 1.\n$$\n\nEvaluate $\\displaystyle{\\int _C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r}$ for $\\mathbf F = ye^{xy}\\,\\mathbf i + xe^{xy}\\,\\mathbf j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/example-8760.json" "file_sha256" "fe5ac9a5157f257a66b0b14fa699b2e9e6f940401b2ad75b592d79d68d74fe1e"} {"question" "e-8760" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $\\mathbf F = \\nabla f.$ Since we're given that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =y{e}^{xy}, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =x{e}^{xy}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)= e^{xy}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $\\nabla f = \\mathbf F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,1⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(1,0).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,0)-f(0,1) \\\\  & ={e}^{1\\cdot 0}-{e}^{0\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =1-1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059357/example-8760.json" "file_sha256" "fe5ac9a5157f257a66b0b14fa699b2e9e6f940401b2ad75b592d79d68d74fe1e"}] "usage" {"e-8760" [] "e-8759" [] "e-9502" [] "q-123879" []} "basis" 1893 "retractions" [[17592186134002 :question/problem "Let $f(x, y) = y^{2} - x^{2}$. Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y^{2} = 4x$ for $y ∈ [0, 2\\sqrt{2}]$, evaluate $∫_{C}∇f \\cdot dr$."] [17592186133617 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=xy^2+ 2xz$. Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $\\mathbf r(t)= \\langle e^{t}, t, e^{-t}\\rangle$ for $t\\in \\left[0,1\\right]$, evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_{C} \\nabla f\\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r$."] [17592186133617 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b]$. Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C$. The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a))\n$$\n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(0) &= ⟨1, 0, 1⟩ \\\\\nr(1) &= ⟨e, 1, \\frac{1}{e}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,0,1)$ and\n\n$$\n\\left(e, 1, \\dfrac{1}{e}\\right)\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{C}∇f \\cdot dr &= f(r(1)) - f(r(0)) \\\\\n&= f(e, 1, \\frac{1}{e}) - f(1, 0, 1) \\\\\n&= (e(1)^{2} + 2 \\cdot e \\cdot \\frac{1}{e}) - (1(0)^{2} + 2(1)(1)) \\\\\n&= (e + 2) - 2 \\\\\n&= e\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186133629 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)= xe^{y}$. Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $x + 2y=3$ for $x\\in [-1,1]$, evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_{C} \\nabla f\\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r$."] [17592186133629 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b]$. Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C$. The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a))\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C$. A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\left\\langle t, \\dfrac{1}{2}(3-t)\\right\\rangle, \\quad t\\in[-1,1]\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(-1) &= ⟨ - 1, 2⟩, \\\\\nr(1) &= ⟨1, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(-1,2)$ and $(1,1)$.\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{C}∇f \\cdot dr &= f(r(1)) - f(r(-1)) \\\\\n&= f(1, 1) - f(-1, 2) \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot e^{1} - (-1) \\cdot e^{2} \\\\\n&= e + e^{2} \\\\\n&= e(1 + e)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186133618 :question/problem "Consider the curve $C$, defined parametrically as\n\n$\\mathbf r(t) = \\left\\langle t, 1-t \\right\\rangle, \\quad 0 \\leq t \\leq 1$.\n\nEvaluate $\\displaystyle{\\int _C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r}$ for $\\mathbf F = ye^{xy}\\,\\mathbf i + xe^{xy}\\,\\mathbf j$.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative.*"] [17592186133618 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b]$. Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C$. The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a))\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F = \\nabla f\n$$\n\nSince we're given that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{x}(x, y) &= ye^{xy}, \\\\\nf_{y}(x, y) &= xe^{xy}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)= e^{xy}\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n\\nabla f = \\mathbf F\n$$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(0) &= ⟨0, 1⟩, \\\\\nr(1) &= ⟨1, 0⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(1,0)$.\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{C}F \\cdot dr &= ∫_{C}∇f \\cdot dr \\\\\n&= f(1, 0) - f(0, 1) \\\\\n&= e^{1 \\cdot 0} - e^{0 \\cdot 1} \\\\\n&= 1 - 1 \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186130576 17592186130577 17592186130578 17592186130579 17592186130580] "immutable_fields" [[17592186130575 :answer-field/correct] [17592186130575 :answer-field/id] [17592186130575 :answer-field/key] [17592186130575 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
